window.BOOK={"parts": [{"label": "Part I", "name": "General Part", "from_": 1, "to": 5}, {"label": "Part II", "name": "Design of a Training Program", "from_": 6, "to": 15}, {"label": "Part III", "name": "Type of Physical Activities", "from_": 16, "to": 25}, {"label": "Part IV", "name": "Recovery and Adherence", "from_": 26, "to": 28}], "chapters": [{"n": 1, "title": "Changes, Modifications, Adaptations of the Maternal Organism During Pregnancy", "summary": "The chapter reviews how each body system adapts to pregnancy and what that means for exercise. These adaptations are described as necessary physiological responses, not pathological ones, and they are not uniform across women or across the pregnancy.\n\nThe cardiovascular, blood, respiratory and metabolic systems all change substantially: heart rate, blood volume, ventilation and energy needs rise, while weight gain targets depend on prepregnancy BMI. The musculoskeletal system shows ligament laxity, a shifted center of gravity, abdominal diastasis and altered gait. Emotional changes vary by trimester, and renal, digestive, skin, nervous, immune and reproductive systems also adapt. The chapter stresses that systems interact, so prenatal exercise is a multisystemic stimulus that should be individualized.", "points": ["Cardiovascular changes begin around the fifth week of gestation and can continue up to a year after delivery.", "Resting heart rate rises by 10-20 beats/min (up to 30%), and blood volume increases by 40-45% with red blood cells up 18-25%, causing physiological anemia of pregnancy.", "Anemia is defined as hemoglobin below 11 g/100 ml; pregnancy also brings hypercoagulability.", "Minute ventilation increases 30-40%, oxygen consumption rises 10%-20%, and the diaphragm rises about 4 cm when standing.", "Basal metabolic rate increases 15%-20%, and energy needs rise by about 300 kcal/day from the second trimester.", "Recommended total weight gain by prepregnancy BMI: under 18.5 is 12.5-18 kg, normal is 11.5-16 kg, overweight is 7-11.5 kg, and obese is 5-9 kg; first trimester gain is 0.5-2.0 kg for all.", "Relaxin and progesterone increase ligament laxity, the center of gravity shifts, the rectus abdominis separates (diastasis), and gait speed, cadence and stride length fall, raising fall risk.", "Emotional patterns differ by trimester: anxiety and tiredness in the first, more energy in the second, insomnia and fear of childbirth in the third.", "Renal plasma flow and glomerular filtration rise about 50%, increasing urinary frequency and infection risk."], "takeaways": ["Combine heart rate monitoring with the Borg scale or the Talk Test, and recalculate heart rate monthly.", "Favor moderate aerobic exercise and avoid maximal or strenuous training.", "Stay hydrated, eat frequent balanced meals, have a snack before training, and avoid training on an empty stomach or prolonged fasting.", "Avoid high-impact exercise, bouncing, excessive stretching, and crunches or prolonged front planks; train balance, lumbopelvic stability and posture.", "Practice slow deep breathing and avoid breath holding (Valsalva); exercise in cool, well-ventilated places with breathable clothing.", "Include urination breaks, start pelvic floor work (adapted Kegel) from the first trimester, and keep moving to avoid sedentary behavior."], "cautions": ["Avoid long periods lying on the back because of the risk of supine hypotension syndrome.", "Avoid prolonged fasting, which can cause maternal hypoglycemia and ketosis that is harmful to the fetus.", "Avoid sudden posture changes, quick turns and sudden movements, and watch for dizziness or exhaustion."]}, {"n": 2, "title": "Maternal Response to Physical Exercise", "summary": "This chapter explains how the pregnant body responds to a single exercise session (acute response) and to regular training over time (chronic adaptation). During exercise the body must keep blood flowing to active muscles, protect uteroplacental blood flow, and regulate temperature and metabolism.\n\nAcute responses include faster heart rate rise, greater cardiac output, deeper breathing, more fat use, better insulin sensitivity and extra heat production. Uteroplacental flow stays stable at moderate intensity, and fetal heart rate may rise 10-20 bpm, returning to baseline within 5 minutes. Chronic training improves cardiovascular efficiency, glucose control, strength, balance and well-being, with benefits for the fetus such as lower risk of macrosomia and cesarean section. The authors conclude that the pregnant body is efficient and adaptable, and that training should be adapted to stage, level and need.", "points": ["Each session must maintain muscle perfusion, protect uteroplacental blood flow and regulate body temperature and metabolism.", "Cardiac output may increase by an additional 30%-40% during exercise, and heart rate rises faster and higher than in nonpregnant women under the same load.", "Maximum heart rate is usually reduced in pregnancy, so it should not be the sole intensity indicator; the Borg scale (RPE 12-14) and the talk test are recommended.", "Prioritize moderate intensities of 50%-70% of VO2max.", "Basal temperature rises slightly (0.3 C-0.5 C), and exercise adds metabolic heat.", "Above 85% of maximum heart rate, uteroplacental flow may be temporarily reduced, so vigorous intensities are not recommended without medical supervision.", "Fetal heart rate may increase 10-20 bpm during maternal exertion and returns to baseline within 5 minutes; no signs of fetal distress were found in healthy women training in a controlled manner.", "Weight-bearing activity such as treadmill walking causes higher VO2 in pregnant women than cycle ergometry.", "Regular exercise improves glycemic and lipid control, strength, balance and well-being, and in the fetus reduces the risk of macrosomia and cesarean section."], "takeaways": ["Use RPE 12-14 and the talk test rather than heart rate alone to set intensity.", "Avoid prolonged fasting before training and have a light intake of complex carbohydrates before and after exercise.", "Train in cool, well-ventilated settings with breathable clothing and good hydration.", "Strengthen core, gluteal and stabilizing muscles, and add proprioception and balance training; avoid jumping and sudden impacts.", "Add conscious breathing work such as yoga or mindfulness, and avoid the Valsalva maneuver.", "Individualize sessions by trimester, check vital signs before and after, include core, pelvic floor and breathing work, and coordinate with an obstetrician or physical therapist."], "cautions": ["Avoid hot or humid environments because of the thermoregulation load.", "Monitor time spent lying on the back, since prolonged supine exercise reduces venous return and uterine flow.", "Avoid Valsalva and other exercises that cause apnea.", "Do not use vigorous intensities above 85% of maximum heart rate without medical supervision."]}, {"n": 3, "title": "What Type of Women Can Become Pregnant and Who to Train with Them? Differences Between Sedentary, Active, and Athlete Women", "summary": "The chapter describes three profiles of pregnant women (sedentary, active and athlete) and explains that prior activity level shapes how pregnancy adaptations occur. It defines each profile and notes that only 23% of pregnant women report meeting recommended activity guidelines, with lower rates in overweight or obese women.\n\nSedentary women tend to have more weight gain, more risk of gestational diabetes and hypertension, and higher anxiety and depression, so they should start gently with low-impact activity. Active women can usually continue their routine at moderate intensity. Athletes can keep training but must adjust intensity, duration and type. All need prior medical evaluation, gradual change and monitoring. The chapter warns that women who train several times a week may still be sedentary the rest of the day, and that every program must be personalized.", "points": ["Only 23% of pregnant women report meeting recommended physical activity guidelines, and the figure is lower for overweight or obese women.", "Pregnant women spend more than 50% of their time in sedentary behaviors, defined as waking behavior at 1.5 METs or less while sitting, reclining or lying.", "A sedentary person does little or no activity, an active person meets international recommendations, and an athlete trains intensely and in a structured way to improve performance.", "Sedentary women show lower muscle tone, higher body fat, lower aerobic capacity, more pronounced weight gain and more risk of gestational diabetes and hypertension.", "Sedentary women should start with walking, swimming or prenatal yoga and accumulate at least 150 minutes of moderate activity per week across several days.", "Muscle-strengthening should be done twice a week for sedentary women, focusing on the core and pelvic floor.", "Active women can continue their usual routine without medical contraindications, at moderate intensity, avoiding high-impact or fall-risk exercise.", "Athletes can continue training but should avoid high-impact exercise, fall-risk activities and positions that may compromise blood flow.", "All women need prior medical evaluation, gradual adaptation, constant monitoring, and education and support."], "takeaways": ["Get a complete medical evaluation before starting or continuing an exercise program.", "Change the routine gradually from your current fitness level.", "Beginners should begin with short sessions of walking, swimming or prenatal yoga and build duration and intensity slowly.", "Aim for 150 minutes of moderate activity a week plus strength work twice a week.", "Active women and athletes should listen to the body, prioritize recovery, and watch for excessive fatigue, pain or discomfort.", "Reduce sitting time all day, even if you train three times a week."], "cautions": ["A complete medical evaluation is essential before any pregnancy exercise program to identify contraindications.", "Avoid high-impact exercises and activities with a risk of falling.", "Athletes should avoid exercise positions that may compromise blood flow."]}, {"n": 4, "title": "Benefits of Physical Exercise for the Mother, Fetus, Newborn, and Infant", "summary": "The chapter argues that older advice to rest during pregnancy has been replaced by evidence that moderate exercise, and in some cases well-supervised higher-intensity exercise, is safe and beneficial in most low-risk pregnancies. Pregnancy is presented as a window of opportunity to build lasting healthy habits.\n\nFor mothers, exercise is linked to fewer cesareans, less gestational diabetes, preeclampsia and gestational hypertension, less excessive weight gain, better mood and sleep, and less back and pelvic pain. For newborns it is linked to healthy birth weight, better Apgar scores and shorter hospital stays. Infant studies suggest benefits for motor and neurodevelopment and lower obesity risk, though some studies at ages 7 and 18 months found no differences. The authors conclude that prenatal exercise in women without contraindications should be part of routine obstetric care, with personalization and supervision.", "points": ["Moderate exercise, and in some cases well-supervised higher-intensity exercise, is safe and beneficial in most low-risk pregnancies.", "Maternal benefits include fewer cesarean deliveries, lower risk of gestational diabetes, preeclampsia and gestational hypertension, and less excessive gestational weight gain.", "Exercise also reduces depressive symptoms, back and pelvic pain, fatigue and stress, improves sleep, helps prevent and manage urinary incontinence, and reduces postpartum weight retention.", "Lack of exercise during pregnancy increases the risk of a macrosomic fetus by 2.5 times.", "Newborns of active mothers have lower fat mass at birth, better Apgar scores and shorter neonatal hospital stays.", "Excess birth weight raises risks such as hypoglycemia and shoulder dystocia, while birth weight under 2500 g is linked to developmental complications and later chronic disease.", "At 5 days, 1 month and 1 year, studies report better neurobehavioral capacity, lower adiposity, higher heart rate variability and better neuromotor development in children of exercising mothers.", "At age 7 there was no association between maternal exercise and child weight or neurodevelopment, and one study found no motor skill difference at 18 months.", "The most recent evidence (2020-2025) confirms exercise in women without contraindications is safe, with no increased risk of preterm birth."], "takeaways": ["Treat pregnancy as a chance to establish exercise habits that may last a lifetime.", "Regular exercise is a practical tool for gestational weight control and lowering the risk of gestational diabetes and hypertension.", "Include pelvic floor training to help prevent and manage urinary incontinence.", "Expect mood, sleep, and back and pelvic pain to benefit from staying active.", "Exercise can help keep birth weight in a healthy range, which matters for both the mother and the baby.", "Personalize and supervise the program, and treat prenatal exercise as part of routine obstetric care."], "cautions": ["The benefits apply to women without contraindications and in low-risk pregnancies.", "Higher-intensity exercise is only described as acceptable when well supervised."]}, {"n": 5, "title": "International Consensus on Physical Exercise. How Do I Interpret the Clinical Guidelines?", "summary": "This chapter compares the main international guidelines on exercise in pregnancy: WHO, ACOG, CSEP/SOGC, RCOG, RANZCOG and SEGO. Research over the past 50 years offers a reassuring margin of safety, and the guidelines agree that regular, safe exercise is part of prenatal care. They differ in terminology, risk classification, recommended intensity and level of evidence, so knowing those differences helps apply them in context.\n\nAll guidelines recommend at least 150 min of moderate-intensity activity per week, spread over several days, built on aerobic and muscle-strengthening work. They agree on absolute and relative contraindications, avoiding prolonged supine positions after week 20, and avoiding falls or abdominal trauma. Differences reflect method, local culture and clinical emphasis rather than contradiction. The chapter also lists limits, such as little evidence on high-intensity exercise in high-risk pregnancies, and treats guidelines as a flexible framework.", "points": ["Healthy pregnant women are advised to do 150 min of moderate physical activity at least 3 days a week, ideally with professional supervision.", "WHO allows 150 min of moderate-intensity or 75 min of high-intensity activity per week, and advises avoiding prolonged inactivity.", "ACOG advises avoiding supine exercise after the 20th week, hot and humid environments, and activities with a risk of falling or abdominal trauma.", "CSEP/SOGC, RCOG, RANZCOG and SEGO all recommend at least 150 min of moderate-intensity activity per week, spread through the week.", "Vigorous intensity is allowed by RCOG and RANZCOG only if the woman was previously active; RCOG is more conservative than ACOG.", "Priority goes to aerobic activities (walking, swimming, stationary cycling) and core and lower-body strengthening, supplemented by flexibility work.", "Absolute contraindications include cervical incompetence, placenta praevia with active bleeding and uncontrolled preeclampsia; relative ones include multiple pregnancy and maternal cardiovascular disease.", "Documented benefits include gestational weight control, lower risk of gestational diabetes and preeclampsia, better mental health, less musculoskeletal pain and preparation for childbirth.", "Known limitations include limited evidence on high-intensity exercise in high-risk pregnancies and limited long-term data on infant neurocognitive and metabolic development."], "takeaways": ["Aim for at least 150 min of moderate activity per week, spread across several days.", "Check the guidelines of your own country or region for local particularities.", "Adapt the plan to your activity history, health status, obstetric conditions and preferences.", "Use the Borg or a similar perceived exertion scale to set intensity.", "Involve obstetricians, physiotherapists and certified trainers, especially for supervised programs.", "When reading a guideline, check the level of evidence and whether a rule is absolute or relative."], "cautions": ["Avoid exercising on your back for long periods after week 20.", "Avoid hot and humid environments and activities with a risk of falling or abdominal trauma.", "Stop exercising and seek care if warning signs appear, such as bleeding, pain, contractions or dizziness."]}, {"n": 6, "title": "Safe Guidelines Based on International Consensus", "summary": "This chapter turns the international consensus into practical safety guidance. Controlled, adapted exercise is safe and beneficial for mother and fetus, with 150 min of moderate activity per week spread over at least three sessions, preferably daily. It covers special populations, recommended exercise types, how to adapt by trimester, and when to stop a session or the whole program.\n\nLow-intensity exercise in controlled gestational diabetes or mild hypertension improves metabolic control under multidisciplinary supervision, and in obesity, exercise plus nutritional counselling reduces weight gain and macrosomia risk. Tables list warning signs that mean stopping immediately, absolute contraindications (such as severe preeclampsia or placenta praevia with bleeding) and relative ones (such as moderate anemia or multiple pregnancy). Resuming requires gradual progression and obstetrician authorization.", "points": ["The consensus is 150 min of moderate activity per week, spread over at least three sessions per week, preferably daily.", "Suggested frequency is 3-5 days per week, at moderate intensity (12-14 on the Borg scale or the talk test), in 30-55-min sessions.", "Inactive women should start gradually with 10-15 min daily, while previously active women can keep vigorous training under professional supervision.", "Recommended activities include walking, swimming, stationary cycling, gentle jogging, light dancing, bands or light weights, prenatal yoga and adapted Pilates.", "Avoid impact, risk of falling or abdominal trauma, for example contact sports, horse riding or skiing.", "By trimester: first, progressive adaptation and pelvic floor; second, avoid supine exercise and work lumbar-pelvic stability; third, reduce impact and intensity and favor aquatic or low-impact exercise.", "Stop a session immediately for severe dyspnea, chest pain, dizziness, vaginal bleeding, fluid leakage, regular contractions, persistent abdominal pain or a sudden decrease in fetal movements.", "Absolute contraindications include severe preeclampsia (hypertension >=160/110 mmHg), placenta previa with active bleeding (>26 weeks), PROM and severe anemia (Hb < 9 g/dL).", "Relative contraindications include moderate anemia (Hb 9-11 g/dL), uncontrolled diabetes, BMI > 40 or < 18.5, and multiple pregnancy at risk of preterm delivery."], "takeaways": ["Use the talk test: you should be able to hold a fluent conversation without shortness of breath.", "Start with 10-15 min daily if you were inactive, and build up gradually.", "Drink water before, during and after exercise and avoid hot environments.", "Choose low-impact or aquatic options as the third trimester advances.", "Keep activity logs and regular medical monitoring alongside education.", "After a problem resolves, resume at low intensity and short duration, with obstetrician approval."], "cautions": ["If a warning sign appears, stop immediately, adopt a semi-sitting or left side lying position, hydrate and report to a healthcare professional; get urgent obstetric evaluation if symptoms persist.", "Avoid prolonged supine positioning in the second and third trimesters, as it can reduce venous return and cause maternal hypotension.", "With absolute contraindications, suspend exercise completely until medical resolution."]}, {"n": 7, "title": "Initial Assessment and History", "summary": "The initial assessment establishes the pregnant woman's safety, a functional baseline, and the basis for a program adapted to pregnancy. It is interdisciplinary: the obstetrician, a pelvic floor physiotherapist and an exercise science professional work together. Its aims include ruling out contraindications, determining obstetric risk, identifying prior activity habits, assessing pelvic floor and posture, and supporting adherence.\n\nThe assessment is organized into seven blocks: identification, personal and family history, obstetric history, current pregnancy, physical activity history, lifestyle, and medical examination with basic clinical parameters. Tables give normal values, three fitness levels with starting recommendations, laboratory tests, and a four-level risk classification from low to very high, which guides the type, intensity and supervision of exercise.", "points": ["The assessment aims to ensure maternal and fetal safety, estimate obstetric risk and set a functional baseline for load, intensity and type of exercise.", "It is organized into seven blocks, from sociodemographic data through to medical examination and clinical parameters.", "Physical activity history uses tools such as PARmed-X or the PPAQ to classify the woman as active, moderately active or sedentary.", "Normal blood pressure in pregnancy is < 140/90 mmHg, resting heart rate 70-90 bpm (may rise to 100), respiratory rate 12-20 per minute and body temperature <= 37.5 C.", "Prepregnancy BMI of 18.5-24.9 kg/m2 is the reference range, and expected gestational weight gain is 9-18 kg depending on BMI.", "Fetal heart rate is normally 110-160 bpm, and peripheral edema should be absent or mild.", "Fitness is low, moderate or high: low starts with gentle walking, breathing and pelvic floor work; moderate maintains 150 min/week; high continues an adapted program prioritizing stability and fetal safety.", "Lab references include hemoglobin 11-14 g/dL, fasting glucose 70-95 mg/dL and serum ferritin 20-150 ng/mL.", "Risk is classed low, moderate, high or very high/absolute, and the last means discontinuing exercise until medical clearance."], "takeaways": ["Have the obstetrician rule out contraindications before you start.", "Gather your obstetric history, current pregnancy data and prior activity level before planning.", "Record baseline vital signs and fitness results so you can compare across trimesters.", "Match your starting program to your fitness level: low, moderate or high.", "Keep a structured record shared among doctor, physiotherapist and trainer.", "Include pelvic floor, lumbopelvic stability and posture in the assessment."], "cautions": ["Avoid exercise if there is fever or acute infection.", "Sustained blood pressure elevations suggest gestational hypertension or incipient preeclampsia and need attention.", "With absolute contraindications (preeclampsia, placental abruption, placenta previa with bleeding, rupture of membranes), stop exercise until medical clearance."]}, {"n": 8, "title": "Goal Settings", "summary": "Setting goals defines the direction of an exercise program, makes results measurable and raises adherence. Goals should be clear, individualized, evidence-based, and can target safety and prevention, functional optimization, metabolic and cardiovascular health, psychological well-being and preparation for childbirth. The chapter adapts the SMART model to pregnancy, for example raising the 30-s chair stand test from 10 to 14 repetitions.\n\nAdherence is limited by physical, psychological and social factors, so the chapter applies four motivation frameworks: Self-Determination Theory, the Theory of Planned Behavior, the Transtheoretical Model of Change and positive reinforcement with social learning. Practical applications include letting the woman choose her activity, tracking progress, involving family, matching advice to her stage of change, and giving feedback. The aim is greater adherence and lower dropout.", "points": ["Goals can target safety and prevention, functional optimization, metabolic and cardiovascular health, psychological well-being and childbirth preparation.", "SMART means Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant and Time-bound, for example improving the 30-s chair stand test from 10 to 14 repetitions.", "Self-Determination Theory says behaviors last when the person feels autonomy, competence and relatedness.", "Autonomy means choosing the activity (walking, swimming, prenatal yoga), while competence comes from achievable goals recorded in a tracking table.", "The Theory of Planned Behavior links intention to attitudes, social support and perceived control, so educate, involve partner or family, and teach safe progression with RPE.", "The Transtheoretical Model has five stages: precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action and maintenance, each with matched interventions.", "An action-stage example is a 30-min aerobic plus strength program started twice a week, with progress recorded.", "Positive reinforcement includes feedback, group programs and tracking, such as a woman improving from 8 to 10 squats in 40 s and getting a new goal of 12.", "Progression should be monitored with functional tests and RPE, adjusting intensity to the trimester."], "takeaways": ["Write SMART goals with a measurable number and a deadline, such as before the third trimester.", "Choose activities you enjoy and can adapt to your comfort level.", "Keep a tracking table of repetitions, distance or RPE to see progress.", "Bring partner, family or a group into your routine for support.", "Find out which stage of change you are in and ask for matching support.", "Ask your professional to adjust goals with feedback as you improve and as the trimester changes."], "cautions": []}, {"n": 9, "title": "Physical Tests", "summary": "Physical assessment before starting an exercise program lets a trainer personalize intensity, duration and type of activity. Tests must be safe, validated and adapted to the physiological, biomechanical and hormonal changes of pregnancy. The chapter notes that many common tests are too intense for pregnant women, and that the literature is inconclusive about which protocols to use.\n\nThe chapter focuses on low-cost field tests, since laboratory tests are the gold standard but costly. It reviews tests for cardiorespiratory fitness, strength, flexibility, speed and balance, and says pelvic floor assessment belongs to physiotherapists. It concludes that a pregnancy-specific, validated test battery is needed, because most current tests are adaptations from other populations.", "points": ["A systematic review found fitness during pregnancy was measured with 75 cardiorespiratory protocols, 16 muscular, 13 flexibility and 40 balance protocols, often lacking validity and reliability.", "Tests should be repeated every 2-3 months and the data recorded to assess progress, with a warm-up and stable conditions (time, food, rest).", "The 6-Minute Walk Test uses a 30-meter corridor at a comfortable pace, with heart rate monitored and RPE of 12-14 recommended.", "The Modified Step Test uses a 15-20 cm step for 3 min at a controlled pace, measuring heart rate afterward.", "The Modified Balke treadmill protocol (validated by Mottola et al.) suits trained women and athletes without contraindications, but needs a treadmill.", "The Chair Stand Test lasts 30 s without using the arms; handgrip dynamometry gives a general indicator of strength, but a maximal isometric test should not be used.", "In the Modified Sit-and-Reach Test a range greater than 20 cm is adequate; the Back Scratch Test and goniometry assess shoulder and joint mobility.", "Speed testing (10 m timed walk) is not ideal because speed should not be a focus in pregnancy and is linked to sudden movements and fall risk.", "The single-leg stance test is a simple, inexpensive field balance test; no articles on agility and coordination assessment were found."], "takeaways": ["Use submaximal tests and never add risk during testing.", "Sedentary beginners should use the 6MWT or Modified Step Test.", "Trained women without contraindications may use the Modified Balke treadmill protocol if equipment is available.", "Include lower-limb strength tests such as the chair stand test.", "Do balance tests next to a wall or support bar under close supervision.", "Repeat tests every 2-3 months and keep a record."], "cautions": ["Discontinue testing if dizziness, pelvic pain, excessive shortness of breath or contractions occur.", "Avoid prolonged supine positions after the second trimester and avoid bouncing or hyperextension in flexibility tests.", "Pelvic floor assessment (e.g., PERFECT, biofeedback) must be done only by physiotherapists or qualified professionals."]}, {"n": 10, "title": "Validated Questionnaires", "summary": "Questionnaires go beyond physical tests: they allow longitudinal monitoring, are inexpensive, easy to administer and adaptable to different cultures. They quantify usual activity, identify activity domains (recreational, domestic, work, transport), detect sedentary risk and help personalize programs.\n\nThe chapter presents two tools. PARmed-X for Pregnancy screens fitness for exercise and flags contraindications with a green, yellow or red result. The Pregnancy Physical Activity Questionnaire (PPAQ) quantifies activity in MET-hours per week. Used together with SMART goals and repeated each trimester, they support safe, individualized exercise prescription.", "points": ["Questionnaires are useful for quantifying usual activity, identifying activity domains, detecting sedentary behavior and complementing clinical and functional assessments.", "PARmed-X for Pregnancy covers demographic and obstetric data, medical history, recent symptoms (chest pain, dizziness, bleeding, contractions) and activity level.", "PARmed-X results: green means exercise without restrictions, yellow means supervised or cautious exercise, red means contraindicated until medical evaluation.", "The PPAQ covers recreational, work-related, transit and domestic activity, scored in METs x hours/week.", "PPAQ categories: sedentary is <7.5 MET-h/week, moderately active 7.5-14, and active >14 MET-h/week.", "The suggested sequence is PPAQ for baseline, PARmed-X for risk classification, SMART goals, then monitoring.", "For a sedentary woman, start with 10-15 min walks per day, progressing to a minimum of 150 min per week.", "Questionnaires should be readministered each trimester to adjust intensity and volume."], "takeaways": ["Apply the PPAQ first to record baseline activity and define the type of woman being trained.", "Use PARmed-X to identify absolute or relative contraindications before programming.", "Set SMART goals collaboratively based on the woman's profile.", "Active women can maintain activity, slightly increase strength training and add core and pelvic floor work.", "Athletic women should adapt previous activity to moderate activity without impact or risks.", "Share questionnaire data with obstetricians, physical therapists and exercise specialists."], "cautions": ["A red PARmed-X result means exercise is contraindicated until medical evaluation."]}, {"n": 11, "title": "Frequency", "summary": "Training frequency is the number of exercise sessions in a given period, usually days per week. The recommended frequency depends on prior fitness and stage of pregnancy. International consensus says healthy pregnant women should do at least 150 min of moderate-intensity activity per week, spread over three or more days, ideally every day.\n\nClinical guidelines advise moderate activity 3-5 days a week, which gives significant benefit without compromising maternal or fetal health, and greater adherence leads to better outcomes. The chapter gives frequencies by trimester and by profile (sedentary, active, athlete), and stresses listening to the body and allowing recovery.", "points": ["Healthy pregnant women should perform at least 150 min of moderate-intensity activity per week, over three or more days, ideally every day.", "Guidelines advise moderate activity 3-5 days a week.", "Greater adherence to and frequency of training lead to better maternal-fetal outcomes.", "In the first trimester, without complications, women can keep their prepregnancy frequency and adjust intensity to how they feel.", "In the second and third trimesters, keep 3-5 days per week, prioritizing low-impact exercise and avoiding fall or abdominal trauma risks.", "Sedentary women can start with low-intensity sessions two to three times per week.", "Active women can continue three to four times per week if there are no contraindications.", "Pregnant athletes can train 5-7 days a week, with constant medical supervision and adjustments as pregnancy progresses."], "takeaways": ["Use 150 min per week of moderate activity across 3 or more days as the baseline.", "Sedentary women should begin gradually, 2-3 sessions per week.", "Choose low-impact activities in the second and third trimesters.", "Plan recovery between sessions to avoid excessive fatigue.", "Adjust frequency to individual needs, trimester and prior fitness level.", "Combine regular activity with sufficient rest."], "cautions": ["Stop exercising immediately if dizziness, bleeding, chest pain or difficulty breathing appear.", "Avoid overexertion and activities with risk of falls or abdominal trauma."]}, {"n": 12, "title": "Intensity", "summary": "Intensity refers to the perceived exertion during physical activity, and adjusting it is crucial for the safety of mother and fetus. It should be adapted each trimester, especially in the second and third, because of changes in balance and center of gravity.\n\nThree methods are described: the Borg RPE scale, the speech test and heart rate (percentage of heart rate reserve, via the Karvonen formula). Combining them gives the most complete picture. Moderate activities are recommended, high intensity is to be avoided especially by inexperienced women, and advice differs for sedentary, active and athletic women.", "points": ["The Borg RPE scale should be kept between 12 and 14, which corresponds to moderate effort, and remains useful when heart rate is affected by pregnancy.", "The speech test: the woman should hold a conversation without difficulty; if out of breath to speak, intensity is too high.", "The speech test is the most subjective of the three methods.", "Moderate intensity corresponds to approximately 40-60% of heart rate reserve, generally aligned with RPE 12-14, calculated with the Karvonen formula.", "Heart rate percentages should be recalculated at minimum each trimester, ideally monthly, or exceptionally weekly.", "Moderate activities include walking, swimming, stationary cycling, prenatal yoga, Pilates and muscle strengthening; flexibility and relaxation are low intensity.", "Sedentary women should start with gentle walking or water aerobics, keeping RPE between 12 and 13.", "Active women can continue moderate activity such as gentle cycling, swimming or prenatal yoga at RPE 12-14.", "Athletes could partially maintain high-intensity training adapted to pregnancy, while protecting uterine blood flow and preventing dehydration."], "takeaways": ["Combine RPE, the speech test and heart rate monitoring for a comprehensive view.", "Aim for RPE 12-14 (12-13 for sedentary women).", "Use the talk test when no other tools are available.", "Recalculate heart rate targets at least each trimester.", "Choose moderate activities like walking, swimming, cycling or prenatal yoga.", "Athletes should monitor heart rate and vital signs constantly."], "cautions": ["High-intensity exercise should be avoided, especially by women with no prior experience, as it can increase risk of dizziness, fainting or decreased uterine blood flow.", "Avoid exercises that risk abdominal trauma or falls.", "Athletes must prevent dehydration and not compromise uterine blood flow."]}, {"n": 13, "title": "Duration of Sessions", "summary": "The chapter says each session should last long enough to give benefits without causing excessive fatigue. Appropriate duration supports cardiovascular capacity, helps control pregnancy weight, eases lower back pain, sciatica and muscle tension, and improves mood, energy and stress.\n\nAerobic sessions of 20-30 min add up to at least 150 min of moderate activity per week, while strength and flexibility work can be done in 15-20-min sessions two to three times per week. Duration should be adjusted by trimester and by the woman's profile (sedentary, active, athlete), staying flexible according to tolerance and symptoms. Excessive fatigue is a sign to reduce duration or intensity.", "points": ["Aerobic sessions are suggested at 20-30 min, accumulating at least 150 min of moderate activity per week.", "Strength and flexibility exercises can be done in 15-20-min sessions, two to three times per week.", "In the first trimester, women could start with shorter sessions and gradually lengthen them according to tolerance.", "In the second and third trimesters, avoid prolonged sessions without rest, especially long periods of standing.", "Sedentary women should start with 15-20-min sessions and build to 30 min, adding 5 min each week up to 30 or 40 min.", "Sedentary sessions can be split into 10-15-min blocks if needed to support adherence.", "Active women can maintain 30-45-min sessions and should alternate strength and aerobic days if not doing multi-component sessions.", "Pregnant athletes can maintain 45-60-min sessions, divided into aerobic and strength blocks with adequate rest and active recovery.", "Duration should be flexible, depending on tolerance, symptoms and trimester."], "takeaways": ["Aim for 20-30-min aerobic sessions toward the 150 min weekly total.", "If you are new to exercise, start at 15-20 min and add 5 min per week.", "Split sessions into 10-15-min blocks when that helps you keep going.", "Alternate strength and aerobic days to avoid fatigue when not combining them.", "Take rests and avoid long periods on your feet in later trimesters.", "Treat heavy fatigue as a cue to shorten or ease sessions."], "cautions": ["Excessive fatigue indicates duration or intensity should be reduced; consult a healthcare professional if necessary.", "Avoid prolonged sessions without rest in the second and third trimesters."]}, {"n": 14, "title": "Volume", "summary": "Volume is the total amount of exercise performed, considering frequency, intensity and duration. The chapter links adequate physical activity to a lower risk of gestational diabetes, preeclampsia and pregnancy-induced hypertension, better mood and energy, easier childbirth, and better postpartum recovery and weight management.\n\nThe general goal is at least 150 min of moderate-intensity activity per week in 20-30-min sessions on 3-5 days, combining aerobic, strength and flexibility work. Volume is then adjusted to prior activity level and health status, with specific ranges for sedentary, active and athlete women. Complications such as hypertension or gestational diabetes require adjusting volume under medical supervision.", "points": ["Volume means the total exercise performed, combining frequency, intensity and duration.", "The weekly goal is at least 150 min of moderate-intensity activity, in 20-30-min sessions on 3-5 days per week.", "Programs should combine aerobic, strength and flexibility exercises.", "Active women can keep a similar volume to before pregnancy, while sedentary women start lower and increase gradually.", "With hypertension or gestational diabetes, volume must be adjusted under medical supervision.", "Sedentary women are advised 90-150 min of moderate activity per week over 3-5 days, increasing with tolerance.", "Active women can maintain a minimum of 150-300 min per week, without neglecting strength, balance, coordination and mobility.", "Athletes can maintain 300 min or more per week, avoiding competitions or extreme training, with active recovery and vital sign monitoring.", "Strength and core work should be moderate and progressive, and is especially important for sedentary women to prevent lower back pain and improve pelvic function."], "takeaways": ["Spread activity over 3-5 days per week, depending on tolerance and trimester.", "Build toward 150 min of moderate activity weekly.", "Mix aerobic, strength and flexibility work.", "Sedentary women should start with 90-150 min per week and increase slowly.", "Add moderate, progressive strength and core training.", "Review volume with your medical team as the pregnancy progresses."], "cautions": ["With conditions such as hypertension or gestational diabetes, adjust volume only under medical supervision.", "Athletes should avoid competitions or extreme training and keep constant medical supervision."]}, {"n": 15, "title": "Material Resources", "summary": "Appropriate equipment can improve the safety and effectiveness of exercise in pregnancy. The chapter lists the most useful and cost-effective conventional items, such as Swiss balls, resistance bands, TRX, light weights, mats, treadmills, step platforms and foam rollers, and notes that any other safe equipment adding resistance is welcome.\n\nIt also lists improvised items like water bottles, towels, sturdy chairs and light backpacks. The key message is that it matters less what you use than how you use it, prioritizing safety and technique. Equipment should be stable, clean and used under qualified supervision, and introduced gradually, especially for sedentary women.", "points": ["Swiss balls suit balance, core strengthening and childbirth preparation.", "Resistance bands give low-impact, adjustable resistance for arms, legs and core.", "Other conventional options include TRX, light weights, barbells (with caution), mats, step ergometer, treadmill, step platform, light medicine balls, coordination ladder and foam roller.", "Unconventional options include lightweight sandbags, water bottles or jugs, towels as bands, tennis balls, steps or boxes, modified jump ropes, sturdy chairs and lightweight backpacks.", "Safety and technique matter more than which equipment is used.", "Equipment should be stable, suitable for pregnancy and kept clean to prevent infection.", "Using equipment under a healthcare professional or qualified trainer is recommended.", "Sedentary women should wear comfortable clothing and proper footwear, start without added weight, and avoid uneven surfaces.", "Athletes can use specialized equipment with constant supervision, adapting high-intensity exercises to safer versions."], "takeaways": ["Use what you have: bottles, towels and sturdy chairs can work as resistance or support.", "Check that equipment is stable and in good condition before each use.", "Clean equipment to prevent infection.", "Learn technique first with no added weight if you are a beginner.", "Exercise on stable, even surfaces to lower fall risk.", "Wear comfortable clothes and appropriate footwear."], "cautions": ["Use only stable equipment suitable for pregnancy.", "Avoid uneven surfaces that could increase the risk of falls.", "Use barbells only with caution and proper technique."]}, {"n": 16, "title": "Endurance", "summary": "Endurance training is presented as a necessity in pregnancy. Pregnancy changes the cardiovascular, respiratory and other systems, and oxygen uptake capacity decreases toward the third trimester, so aerobic training helps optimize cardiovascular function and reduce the burden on the body. Benefits include weight control, better cardiorespiratory capacity, lower gestational diabetes risk, better blood pressure, mood and labor preparation.\n\nThe chapter describes continuous low-to-moderate aerobic exercise, adapted intervals, combined circuits, aquatic exercise and adapted HIIT for active women. It gives intensity targets of 40-60% of heart rate reserve and RPE 12-14, three sample programs for sedentary, active and athlete women, trimester adjustments, and advice to progress gradually with professional supervision.", "points": ["Intensity should stay at 40-60% of heart rate reserve and RPE 12-14, adjusted each trimester.", "Continuous aerobic exercise runs 20-60 min at a steady pace (walking, cycling, swimming, elliptical, water aerobics), using the talk test.", "Intervals for previously active women can be 1 min of effort followed by 2 min of recovery, limited to 80% of usual perceived exertion.", "Combined aerobic and strength circuits are described as the most beneficial modality for maternal health.", "Water exercise gives a lower maternal heart rate for the same oxygen consumption than land-based activity.", "Adapted HIIT suits active women or athletes, using 30-40 s of work and 15-20 s of rest, for example two blocks of 8 sets of 30 s work and 20 s rest.", "The sedentary program is 3 sessions per week, progressing from 20 to 30-35 min over 6-8 weeks at 30-50% of heart rate reserve and RPE 10-13, reaching at least 150 min per week.", "The active program is 4-5 sessions per week of 30 progressing to 40-45 min at RPE 12-14, with 150-180 min per week; the athlete program is 5-6 sessions of 40 progressing to 55-60 min, with 200-250 min per week.", "Progress by increasing duration, then frequency, and finally intensity, and split sessions (for example two 15-min sets) if fatigued."], "takeaways": ["Use the talk test and RPE 12-14 to set effort.", "Pick low-impact options such as walking, cycling, elliptical or swimming, especially in later trimesters.", "Progress step by step with the weekly session-length plan for your profile.", "Split a 30-min session into two 15-min blocks when tired.", "Combine aerobic work with moderate strength.", "Stay hydrated, avoid heat, and adjust each trimester with your professional team."], "cautions": ["Avoid lying on your back from the second trimester onward because of inferior vena cava compression.", "Stop and watch for warning signs such as dizziness, bleeding, contractions, excessive shortness of breath or abdominal pain.", "Avoid maximal efforts, high-impact exercise, jumping and rapid changes of direction; avoid prolonged anaerobic exercise, which can cause hyperthermia and dehydration."]}, {"n": 17, "title": "Strength", "summary": "Strength training in pregnancy, once worried over for safety, is presented as useful when supervised, done with moderate loads and good technique. It supports the extra muscular demands of pregnancy (added body weight, a shifted center of gravity, looser ligaments and the need for postural stability), helps with back pain and posture, and prepares the body for labor and postpartum recovery.\n\nThe chapter describes exercise types: moderate-load work with modifications, strength endurance, bodyweight and isometric stabilization, and aquatic training. It stresses progressive overload aimed at maintenance and function rather than maximal strength, combined with aerobic work and adjusted by trimester. Three sample second-trimester programs are given for sedentary, active and advanced women, and all training should follow a medical evaluation.", "points": ["In a supervised 12-week low-to-moderate program of 32 pregnant women, strength rose 36% (leg press), 39% (leg curl), 41% (lumbar extension) and 56% (leg extension), and lumbar endurance about 14%.", "In that study no musculoskeletal injuries occurred, adverse symptoms were under 2% of sessions, and blood pressure stayed unchanged.", "Strength training may help manage excessive gestational weight gain, improve insulin sensitivity and glucose profile, and reduce gestational diabetes risk, though direct evidence for strength alone is less extensive.", "Strength endurance work uses 12-20 repetitions, two to three sets, with bands or 1-5 kg weights, emphasizing core, glutes, lower back and legs.", "Isometric work uses 20-30 repetitions or 30-40 s holds, for example modified planks, bird dog and glute bridge with pause.", "Modifications include goblet squats instead of back squats, incline or seated presses instead of flat bench in later trimesters, and avoiding Olympic lifts without experience and supervision.", "Avoid prolonged supine positions after the first trimester and limit Valsalva maneuvers.", "The second trimester is usually most favorable; in the third, reduce maximum loads, increase stability and watch balance.", "Sample programs: sedentary 2-3 sessions/week, 20-30 min, RPE 11-13; active 3-4 sessions, 30-40 min, RPE 12-14; advanced 4-5 sessions, 40-60 min, RPE 13-15."], "takeaways": ["Get a medical evaluation and individual adjustments before starting.", "Start with light-to-moderate loads, prioritize technique and progress by tolerance.", "Aim for maintenance and functionality, not maximal strength or hypertrophy.", "Build the week around basic patterns: vertical and horizontal pulls and pushes, hip-dominant and knee-dominant movements.", "Combine strength with aerobic exercise, and include recovery, mobility and core days.", "Beginners can progress to three sets, 15-20 repetitions, and add light weight after 4-6 weeks."], "cautions": ["Avoid excessive Valsalva maneuvers and excessive strain, and keep hydrated and monitor heart rate.", "Avoid prolonged supine positions after the first trimester and exercises that compress the abdomen or reduce venous return.", "Watch for warning signs and fatigue; avoid high-impact exercise and Olympic lifts without experience and supervision."]}, {"n": 18, "title": "Core Training", "summary": "The core is the group of muscles stabilizing the trunk: abdominals, lower back, pelvis, hips, pelvic floor and diaphragm. In pregnancy, core training is not about traditional abs but about strengthening the body's supporting structure to improve function, reduce discomfort, prepare for childbirth and aid postpartum recovery. The transversus abdominis and pelvic floor are key for preventing or managing diastasis recti.\n\nThe chapter covers benefits (posture, back and pelvic pain, pelvic floor and pressure control, well-being), four exercise types (deep activation, stabilizing, dynamic, and water), and what to avoid, such as crunches, sit-ups and abdominal doming or coning. It gives frequency, intensity and a 15-20 minute session structure, trimester adaptations, and advice to integrate core work into a multicomponent program.", "points": ["Include 10 min of core exercises in each session, progressing from isometric to dynamic using a resistance band or Swiss ball.", "Deep core activation uses transverse abdominis and pelvic floor contraction held 5-10 s, with diaphragmatic breathing: inhale relaxing, exhale while activating.", "Modified plank is held 20-30 s and modified side plank 15-20 s per side, starting at 10-15 s if needed.", "Avoid sit-ups, crunches and V-ups, which risk coning, higher intra-abdominal pressure and greater rectus separation.", "Avoid more than 10 min of uninterrupted supine position from the second trimester onward because of vena cava compression.", "Recommended frequency is two to three core sessions per week, 10-20 min each, at moderate RPE 12-14.", "A 15-20 min session has warm-up (3-5 min), deep activation (5 min), postural stability (5-7 min), dynamic work (5-7 min) and cool-down (2-3 min).", "In the third trimester reduce repetitions, use supports, lengthen rests, and prioritize stability over intensity.", "A typical week could be two strength sessions including core, two moderate aerobic sessions, one to two pure core sessions, and daily mobility."], "takeaways": ["Train the deep core (transversus abdominis, pelvic floor, diaphragm) rather than doing sit-ups.", "Coordinate breathing with contraction: exhale while activating.", "Make planks easier by using knees or an elevated support and shorter holds.", "Aim for control, not failure.", "Use supports and a wide, non-slip base for dynamic moves like Pallof press and modified dead bug.", "Fit core work inside a multicomponent program of strength, aerobic and mobility."], "cautions": ["If abdominal doming or coning appears, modify or stop the exercise.", "Avoid prolonged supine positions from the second trimester onward and rapid or large trunk twisting without stability.", "Modify exercises if diastasis recti or other contraindications exist, and avoid excessive abdominal pressure."]}, {"n": 19, "title": "Neuromuscular Coordination and Balance", "summary": "Pregnancy brings a forward-shifting center of gravity, increased uterine volume and weight, ligamentous laxity and gait changes, all of which impair balance and coordination. Training these qualities helps maintain postural control, respond to disturbances, move safely and reduce falls, which matter for both mother and fetus. It also helps women adapt movement patterns, do other exercises with better technique, and feel more confident and consistent.\n\nThe chapter describes static and dynamic balance, functional coordination, stabilization, reaction and aquatic exercises, each with pregnancy adaptations. Programming varies by fitness level, with trimester adjustments and a sample micro-session. Specific evidence in pregnancy is limited, but existing studies and guidelines support including a neuromotor component with supervision and no medical contraindications.", "points": ["Single-leg stance is held 10-30 s and tandem stance 20-30 s, starting at 10 s and increasing to 20 s as tolerated.", "Step-downs use a low step of 20-30 cm with controlled descent.", "Sedentary women train twice a week for 15-20 min at low-moderate intensity.", "Active women train three times a week for 20-30 min at moderate intensity with dynamic and reaction exercises.", "Athletes train three to four times a week for 25-35 min at controlled moderate-high intensity, without high impact.", "Clinical recommendation is two to three sessions per week of 15-30 min, progressing from stable stances to single-leg, unstable surfaces and dynamic movements.", "Sample micro-session: 5 min warm-up, 10-15 min main block (single-leg stance 2x20 s, step-down 2x10, lateral shuffle 2x4 m round trip, bird dog 2x10, eyes-closed single-leg 2x20 s), 3-4 min cool-down.", "In the third trimester keep difficulty moderate, add support points, lower step height, avoid jumps and lengthen rests.", "One concurrent exercise intervention showed improved newborn neuromotor scores at 1 month, though it was not focused only on balance."], "takeaways": ["Progress gradually: bilateral stable stance, single-leg, unstable surface, movement, then eyes closed at advanced level.", "Practice next to a chair, bar or wall for support.", "Use balance work as a warm-up, a separate block or part of the cool-down.", "Keep the space obstacle-free and use safe equipment such as balls, discs and bands.", "Begin with an assessment of balance level, history and any lumbopelvic discomfort.", "Record progress to aid adherence."], "cautions": ["Avoid sudden position changes, jumps and very unstable surfaces without supervision.", "Avoid unstable surfaces if dizziness, loss of control or increased intra-abdominal pressure occur, and avoid prolonged single-leg stance if pelvic pain is present.", "Be alert to obstetric warning signs, and avoid prolonged supine positions and abdominal doming."]}, {"n": 20, "title": "Postural Hygiene, Joint Mobility, Flexibility and Foot Care", "summary": "Pregnancy changes posture and body mechanics: the center of gravity moves forward, relaxin, estrogen and progesterone loosen ligaments, the base of support widens, and breast growth affects the shoulders and neck. These changes raise the risk of low back, sciatic and pelvic pain and fatigue. The chapter presents postural hygiene, joint mobility, flexibility and foot care as pillars for comfort and function.\n\nIt gives practical rules for standing, sitting, getting up and lifting, then mobility exercises for neck, spine, pelvis, hips and shoulders. Stretching guidance stresses gentle, controlled technique, and a foot-care section covers arch flattening, swelling and suitable footwear. Programs are individualized by activity level with supervision.", "points": ["Postural education programs reduced lower back pain intensity by up to 45% in studies cited.", "Wear comfortable shoes with a low heel (2-3 cm) and good cushioning.", "When sitting, change position every 20-30 min, support the back and avoid crossing the legs.", "Avoid lifting loads heavier than 10 kg (22 lbs); bend the knees, keep the back straight and hold the object close.", "To get up, roll to the side, bend the knees and push up with the arms, avoiding sudden or twisting movements.", "Mobility work is daily or 5-6 times a week, 10-20 min, at low intensity without pain, holding 5-10 s for 8-12 repetitions.", "Static stretches are held 20-30 s per muscle with no bouncing or pain and with diaphragmatic breathing.", "Targeted stretches cover hamstrings and glutes, pectorals, adductors and calves.", "Feet may show flattened arches, swelling, altered gait, and on average 5 mm more length after a first pregnancy."], "takeaways": ["Keep weight evenly on both feet and avoid standing for long periods.", "Use a small lumbar pillow if needed when sitting.", "Do a 10-20 min mobility routine as a morning warm-up or evening cool-down.", "Do foot exercises: ankle circles, towel grasping, arch raises and plantar self-massage with a soft ball.", "Choose shoes with flexible soles, stable heel counters and anatomical insoles; avoid high heels or unsupported flats.", "Sedentary women can start with 15 min sessions of light mobility and breathing stretches."], "cautions": ["Avoid forced stretches, bouncing and overstretching, because looser tissues raise injury risk.", "Stop stretching if you experience dizziness, pain or muscle contractions.", "Mobility exercises must be controlled because relaxin and progesterone reduce joint stability."]}, {"n": 21, "title": "Pelvic Floor Muscle Training", "summary": "The pelvic floor is a network of muscles, fasciae, and ligaments that supports the bladder, uterus, and rectum, helps control urine and stool, and contributes to sexual function and lumbopelvic stability. Pregnancy overloads it through uterine weight, hormonal changes (relaxin and estrogens), and posture and breathing changes, and vaginal delivery can stretch the muscles and damage nerves.\n\nPelvic floor muscle training (PFMT) is described as the first line of noninvasive prevention and treatment. It targets strength, endurance, fast contractions, coordination, and relaxation. The chapter sets out a four-phase program, example exercises, adaptations by fitness level, and precautions, and ends by stressing education, supervision, and daily routines to keep women adhering to the program.", "points": ["The pelvic floor has three muscle layers: the deep pelvic diaphragm (levator ani), the middle urogenital diaphragm, and the superficial perineal layer.", "Estimated prevalence of dysfunction in pregnancy and postpartum: urinary incontinence 30-50%, pelvic organ prolapse 10-20%, fecal incontinence 4-10%, pelvic pain or dyspareunia 20-40%.", "Strength training uses maximum sustained contractions of 3-10 s, 8-12 repetitions, two to three sets a day.", "Endurance training uses submaximal contractions of 20-40 s; fast contractions are sets of 10 lasting 1-3 s.", "Phase 1 (weeks 1-4) is awareness: 3-s contractions with 6 s relaxation, 10 repetitions, twice a day.", "Phase 2 (weeks 5-12) is 8-s maximum contractions, 10 repetitions, 3 sets, plus 10 sets of 2-s fast contractions, 5-7 days a week.", "Phase 3 (second and third trimesters) applies contractions to coughing, lifting, and position changes, with inhale = relax and exhale = contract; Phase 4 continues daily PFMT for at least 6 months postpartum.", "An initial assessment with the PERFECT test (Power, Endurance, Repetitions, Fast, Every Contraction Timed) is recommended, with strength graded 0 to 5.", "Sedentary women focus on awareness and short contractions, active women do three to five sets a day, and athletes prioritize relaxation and avoid perineal overtraining."], "takeaways": ["Start PFMT in the first trimester and train daily for 10-15 min.", "Practice relaxing the pelvic floor as much as contracting it.", "Breathe in to relax and breathe out to contract, and combine with diaphragmatic breathing.", "Contract before lifting weights or coughing.", "Avoid compensating with the glutes or abdomen when learning the contraction.", "Continue postpartum for at least 6-12 months."], "cautions": ["Consult your obstetrician if there is a threat of preterm labor or placenta previa.", "Avoid PFMT with acute pelvic pain or uterine contractions during exercise.", "Avoid PFMT with vaginal infections or unhealed postpartum wounds."]}, {"n": 22, "title": "Directed Activities in a Fitness Room", "summary": "Pregnant women without medical contraindications should do at least 150 min of moderate-intensity activity per week, following ACOG guidance. Group fitness classes offer a safe, social, and motivating setting, but each one must be adapted to the trimester, the woman's prior fitness, and pregnancy's physiological changes.\n\nThe chapter goes through Pilates, yoga, indoor cycling, HIIT, Body Pump, Body Combat, aquafitness, functional training, Hyrox, CrossFit, and choreographed classes. For each it lists key adaptations, an example session, and benefits. Common themes are avoiding impact, prolonged lying on the back, and overheating, keeping effort moderate (RPE 12-14), and having trained professionals guide the class.", "points": ["At least 150 min per week of moderate-intensity activity is recommended for women without contraindications.", "Prenatal Pilates is best 2-3 days a week, avoids prolonged supine work from the second trimester, and favors quadruped, seated, or standing positions.", "Prenatal yoga avoids inverted postures and deep twists and emphasizes relaxation and visualization in the third trimester.", "Indoor cycling uses a cadence of 60-80 rpm, heart rate at 40-60% of maximum (or RPE 12-14), no sprints, and 2 days a week.", "HIIT stays at RPE 12-14 with short intervals, long recovery, six to eight rounds of 20-40 s, and no jumping or sudden direction changes.", "Group strength classes reduce load to 40-60% of usual weight and avoid the Valsalva maneuver; CrossFit loads are 50-60% of 1RM.", "Water reduces body weight by up to 50%; aquafitness is best 2-3 days a week.", "Functional training uses light equipment such as bands, a ball, and weights under 5 kg; Hyrox keeps distances and times to 50-60% of standard and replaces running with brisk walking or cycling.", "The chapter states group exercise reduces pregnancy complication risk by up to 40%, and that classes also reduce anxiety, depression, and lower back and pelvic pain."], "takeaways": ["Choose low-impact options and avoid jumps, sudden turns, and explosive moves.", "Keep effort at RPE 12-14 and stay hydrated in a well-ventilated space.", "Reduce weights substantially and breathe out during exertion.", "After the first trimester, avoid long periods on your back and use side-lying, seated, or standing positions.", "Build sessions with a warm-up, a main block, and a cool-down with stretching and breathing.", "Use trained instructors and tailor the class to your level; Hyrox and CrossFit suit very active women or athletes only."], "cautions": ["Avoid overheating during cycling, CrossFit, and other intense classes.", "Avoid exercises that raise abdominal pressure or strain the linea alba, such as burpees, crunches, sit-ups, toes-to-bar, and kipping.", "Hyrox-style work should be done only by very active women or athletes who know the proper technique."]}, {"n": 23, "title": "Relaxation Activities", "summary": "Pregnancy brings hormonal and emotional changes that can cause stress, anxiety, insomnia, muscle tension, and fatigue. The chapter presents relaxation as an essential part of a prenatal exercise program, not an optional extra. It says these practices reduce cortisol, improve heart rate variability, lower prenatal depression and anxiety, and support the mother-baby bond and postpartum recovery.\n\nIt describes eight methods: progressive muscle relaxation, autogenic relaxation, diaphragmatic breathing, mindfulness, positive visualization of birth, massage, aquatic relaxation, and music. Each has an aim, components, and duration. A 60-min sample session and a stage-by-stage guide close the chapter, with an emphasis on a trained professional guiding the practice.", "points": ["Relaxation lowers cortisol and adrenaline while promoting oxytocin and endorphins.", "Listed benefits include lower blood pressure and heart rate, less lower back pain, better sleep, and less anxiety.", "Progressive muscle relaxation (Jacobson) lasts 10-20 min, with arm tension held 5 s in a side-lying or supported seated position.", "Progressive muscle relaxation is reported to significantly reduce stress and improve sleep after 8 weeks of practice.", "Autogenic relaxation (Schultz) sessions run 15-25 min, with initial guided practice from a physiotherapist or psychologist.", "Diaphragmatic breathing uses nasal inhalation, slow exhalation, and a rhythm of four to six breaths per minute.", "Prenatal mindfulness starts with one 60-min session per week, including meditation, body scan, breathing, and visualization.", "Aquatic relaxation takes place in a warm pool at 32-34 degrees C, and music should be soft (60-70 bpm) at moderate volume.", "The 60-min sample session is: breathing 10 min, muscle relaxation 15, body awareness 20, light stretching 10, closing 5."], "takeaways": ["Practice relaxation regularly, not only when stressed.", "Use side-lying or supported seated positions for relaxation work.", "Try slow breathing at four to six breaths per minute.", "Use gentle self-massage on neck, back, and legs, with light pressure and natural oils.", "Play soft music at home before bed to help sleep.", "Early in pregnancy use simple breathing and brief mindfulness; later add muscle relaxation and gentle yoga; near birth add visualization, massage, and music."], "cautions": ["In the third trimester, avoid abdominal tension or breath-holding (apnea) during muscle relaxation.", "In massage, avoid deep abdominal pressure and reflex points on the ankles or wrists.", "Relative contraindications include orthostatic hypotension, breathing difficulties, or emotional hypersensitivity."]}, {"n": 24, "title": "Competitive Sports", "summary": "Pregnancy does not require stopping sport, but competitive athletes face extreme physiological, metabolic, and psychological demands that need case-by-case medical, biomechanical, and psychological review. Whether an athlete can continue depends on prior training level, the type of sport, the trimester, and the obstetric condition, with coordination among gynecologists, physiotherapists, and trainers.\n\nThe chapter sorts sports into generally safe, moderate risk, and contraindicated groups, describes adaptations for each trimester, and gives advice for athletics, swimming, cycling, team sports, and strength sports. It closes with a phased postpartum return plan and a call for clubs and federations to provide flexibility and support.", "points": ["Sports are grouped as generally safe (for example swimming, stationary cycling, light jogging), moderate risk, or contraindicated.", "Moderate-risk sports include competitive running, triathlon, road cycling, team contact sports, combat sports, and high-intensity strength training such as CrossFit or weightlifting.", "Combat sports are contraindicated in competition, though non-contact technical training is possible in the first trimester.", "Contraindicated sports include diving, motorsports, equestrian, climbing, skydiving, paragliding, aggressive alpine skiing, surfing, and mountain biking.", "The first trimester (0-12 weeks) calls for moderate volume, avoiding overheating, and watching for nausea, fatigue, or dizziness.", "The second trimester (13-27 weeks) allows near-normal training with postural adjustments and no prolonged supine exercise.", "The third trimester (28-40 weeks) calls for lower intensity, no impact, and a focus on stabilizing strength and pelvic control.", "Strength athletes should stay preferably below 70% of 1RM and avoid the Valsalva maneuver.", "Postpartum return runs in phases: 0-6 weeks recovery and pelvic floor, 6-12 weeks light aerobic and basic strength, 3-6 months progressive sport-specific load, and no high-intensity competition before 6 months."], "takeaways": ["Plan training with your gynecologist, physiotherapist, and trainer.", "Swap sprints and plyometrics for controlled lower-intensity intervals.", "Favor stationary bikes or rollers over outdoor riding.", "In contact team sports, suspend competition and keep non-contact technical sessions.", "Prioritize technique, breathing, and pelvic control as pregnancy advances.", "Return gradually after birth, with an obstetric evaluation and pelvic floor rehabilitation first."], "cautions": ["Suspend competition in contact team sports during pregnancy.", "Avoid deep diving in swimming because of pressure and fetal oxygenation risks.", "Monitor the placenta, cervix, blood pressure, and blood glucose in athletes who continue."]}, {"n": 25, "title": "Barakat Model", "summary": "The Barakat Model is a scientifically validated session structure that brings aerobic, strength, flexibility, core, balance and pelvic floor work into the same sessions. It is built on six principles: maternal-fetal safety, constant professional supervision, regularity, a multicomponent approach, individualization and educational/motivational guidance.\n\nThe program runs from week 8-10 to week 38-39, three sessions per week of 50-60 min, with the share of each component shifting as pregnancy progresses. Each session has seven parts, from warm-up to a final discussion. The chapter also covers safe body positions, reported maternal and fetal benefits, and the model's limits: it complements, and does not replace, obstetric care.", "points": ["The program runs from week 8-10 to week 38-39 with three sessions per week of 50-60 min.", "Aerobic endurance share falls from 40% until week 20, to 30% until week 30, to 25% until week 38-39.", "Pelvic floor training rises from 10% until week 20, to 15% until week 30, to 20% until week 38-39.", "Strength is 30% until week 20 then 25%; coordination/balance and flexibility/relaxation are 10% then 15%; emotional management is treated every session.", "A session has seven parts: warm-up (5-7 min), moderate aerobic (10-15 min), strength (10-12 min), coordination and balance (7-8 min), pelvic floor (8-10 min), cool-down (5-7 min) and a final discussion.", "Aerobic work is at 55%-65% of heart rate reserve with perceived exertion 12-14 (somewhat hard); strength uses sets of 10-12 repetitions with light dumbbells or bands.", "Supine positioning is limited to 2-3 min to avoid compression of the vena cava; side-lying is an excellent alternative.", "Reported benefits include less excessive weight gain, lower risk of gestational diabetes, preeclampsia and gestational hypertension, less back pain and fatigue, and adequate birth weight with lower macrosomia risk.", "Adherence should exceed 80% to obtain relevant clinical benefits."], "takeaways": ["Aim for three supervised sessions a week of 50-60 min.", "Keep aerobic effort at RPE 12-14 (somewhat hard).", "Include pelvic floor work in every session and increase its share as pregnancy advances.", "Use a stability ball when seated to reduce pelvic pressure.", "Use a modified or side-lying position instead of lying on your back.", "Work with a team such as an obstetrician, physiotherapist and exercise specialist."], "cautions": ["Limit supine positioning to 2-3 min to avoid vena cava compression.", "The model does not replace obstetric medical care.", "Avoid impact activities in the warm-up."]}, {"n": 26, "title": "Recovery Strategies After Physical Exercise", "summary": "Recovery is an essential part of adapting to exercise, and in pregnancy it matters even more because of metabolic, hormonal, biomechanical and emotional changes. Higher plasma volume and cardiac output, elevated resting heart rate, relaxin-related ligament laxity, and higher body temperature all call for more gradual, individualized recovery. The chapter treats recovery as an active phase of training, not a passive period.\n\nIt describes active, passive, nutritional, psychological and physiotherapeutic strategies, then adapts them to aerobic, strength, core/pelvic floor and balance work and to sedentary, active and athlete profiles. It closes with sleep advice and notes that good recovery improves adherence, mood, sleep and preparation for labor.", "points": ["Allow 24-48 h of active recovery between moderate-intensity sessions.", "Monitor for excessive fatigue: RPE >15, dyspnea, dizziness or contractions.", "Active recovery includes slow walking for 5-10 minutes after training, diaphragmatic breathing, light joint mobilization and dynamic stretching.", "Passive recovery includes resting in the left lateral decubitus position and restorative sleep of 7-9 hours.", "Drink 250-300 ml of water or an isotonic beverage immediately after exercise.", "Eat a carbohydrate and protein snack (1:3 ratio) within 30 minutes, for example yogurt with oats, fruit and nuts.", "Fatigue is greater in the first and third trimesters, so reduce session intensity and duration then.", "Psychological tools include 4x4x4 abdominal breathing (inhale 4 s, hold 4 s, exhale 4 s), a 10-minute body scan and restorative prenatal yoga.", "Pregnant athletes recover cardiovascularly well but risk more neuromuscular fatigue if they do not adapt training volume."], "takeaways": ["Finish every session with 5-10 min of slow walking and stretching.", "Drink 250-300 ml of fluid right after exercise and eat a snack within 30 minutes.", "Sleep preferably on your left side, keep a regular schedule, and avoid heavy or sugary meals before bed.", "Use breathing or a short body scan after sessions to lower stress.", "After strength work, use passive stretches or a soft foam roller with controlled breathing.", "Keep rest active: gentle movement, walking and mindful breathing rather than inactivity."], "cautions": ["Watch for RPE >15, dyspnea, dizziness or contractions as signs of excessive fatigue.", "Monitor for symptoms of hypoglycemia or hypotension and avoid prolonged fasting.", "Physiotherapeutic techniques must be done by qualified professionals with medical authorization, avoiding areas of uterine risk."]}, {"n": 27, "title": "Strategies to Increase Adherence. Active Physical Education for Life", "summary": "Adherence is the degree to which a woman consistently, voluntarily and with motivation follows the prescribed activity. In pregnancy it is challenged by physical and hormonal changes, fatigue, nausea, fear, lack of time and lack of support, so it has to be approached as active physical education for life and not only as training design.\n\nThe chapter reviews behavior models, barriers and facilitators, and individual, social and programmatic determinants. Strategies include education, motivational communication, SMART goals, group exercise, flexibility, professional supervision and technology. It also offers profile-specific advice, ways to measure adherence, and evidence of the benefits of high adherence.", "points": ["Barriers are physical (nausea, fatigue, back pain), psychological (fear of harming the baby), social, cultural and economic or logistical.", "Self-efficacy is one of the strongest predictors of behavioral commitment.", "SMART goals are Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant and Time-bound, for example increasing walking from 20 to 30 minutes daily in four weeks.", "Supervised group programs achieve adherence rates exceeding 80%, compared with less than 40% in individual home-based programs.", "Offering Option A (in-person) and Option B (online) for the day's training prevents dropout when a full session is not possible.", "Supervision by a trained specialist is one of the strongest predictors of adherence.", "Digital platforms double the continuity of exercise at home during pregnancy.", "Women active during pregnancy are up to four times more likely to remain active postpartum.", "High adherence to supervised programs reduces gestational diabetes risk by 45%, gestational hypertension by 38% and cesarean likelihood by 25%."], "takeaways": ["Set SMART goals and track them in a log, chart or app.", "Exercise with a group or build a support network, such as a chat group, to stay motivated.", "Keep a backup plan, such as an online version, for low-energy days.", "Learn self-monitoring tools like the Borg scale and talk test and the warning signs.", "Focus on how you feel (energy, mood, less pain) rather than on physical results.", "Sedentary women should start with small goals and gentle exercises; athletes should redefine goals as intensity drops.", "Track attendance weekly, and participation and satisfaction quarterly."], "cautions": ["Technology must be complemented by professional supervision to ensure safety and appropriate intensity."]}, {"n": 28, "title": "Example of a Training Program Plan", "summary": "This chapter gives an indicative, trimester-by-trimester plan for three profiles: sedentary, active and athlete women. Programs should be customized by initial fitness, trimester and any symptoms or contraindications. Design principles include individualization, safe progression (increasing volume, not intensity), variety, body education, professional supervision and effort monitoring at 40%-60% of reserve heart rate and Borg RPE 12-14.\n\nThe trimesters have distinct goals: adapting and building a routine in the first, consolidating strength and function in the second, and maintaining mobility, posture and preparing for childbirth in the third. Tables give weekly content, frequency and intensity for each profile, followed by common components for endurance, strength, core, flexibility and pelvic floor.", "points": ["Weekly frequency is three times for sedentary, four to five for active and five to six for athlete women.", "Session duration is 20-30 min (sedentary), 30-40 min (active) and 45-60 min (athlete).", "Intensity is RPE 11-12 (sedentary), RPE 12-13 (active) and RPE 13-15, no impact (athlete).", "Weekly volume is 90-120 min (sedentary), 150-180 min (active) and 200-250 min (athlete); these figures are indicative and can be modified if a greater load is safely handled.", "The first trimester is weeks 0-13, the second 14-27 and the third 28-40.", "Sedentary women start with light walking 10-20 minutes three times per week at RPE 10-11 in weeks 1-4, reaching 30 minutes walking plus 10 minutes core and pelvic floor at RPE 12 by weeks 9-13.", "Athletes keep 60%-70% of usual volume in weeks 1-4 and reduce to 70% of prepregnancy volume in weeks 14-18.", "Third-trimester intensity drops, for example RPE 10-12 in weeks 37-40 for active women and athletes.", "Common components: endurance 20-45 minutes three to five times per week, and strength of 6-8 exercises, 2-3 sets, 10-15 repetitions."], "takeaways": ["Pick your profile (sedentary, active, athlete) and follow its frequency and duration ranges as a guide.", "Increase volume gradually rather than intensity.", "Use the Borg scale (RPE 12-14) and talk test to self-regulate effort.", "Replace impact with options like elliptical or water aerobics as pregnancy progresses.", "Include core and stability moves such as bridge, bird-dog and cat-cow, plus daily Kegel exercises.", "Prioritize technical control over workload and take regular hydration breaks."], "cautions": ["Avoid hyperthermia (>38 C).", "Avoid prolonged supine positioning from the second trimester onward.", "Avoid anaerobic peaks, high-impact and jumping exercises, and the Valsalva maneuver, as noted in the plan tables."]}]};